 "hypothesis": "Establish a robust, high-throughput-compatible CRISPR-Cas9 knock-in (HDR) system in cell-walled C. reinhardtii as a prerequisite for modular landing pads (Bigge et al. 2026, doi:10.57844/arcadia-61hk-a7gd); test Alcalase as an off-the-shelf substitute for autolysin.",
 "integrity": {
  "contentAddress": "urn:portolan:nrr:sha256:e829e4ef7d8bdeb099c9a020f5495efd163f17e7e49382dd827a137c31036b22",
  "duplicateOf": null,
  "signature": null,
  "signer": null,
  "trustTier": "pilot-unsigned"
 },
 "invalidators": [
  "a milder Alcalase regime (lower dose, shorter time, osmotic support) that preserves viability",
  "a positive editing control showing the guides, donor and selection work in a known-working delivery system",
  "replication with independent reagent batches and experimenters"
 ],
 "kind": "path",
 "license": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/",
 "nextSteps": [
  "Develop a robust, CRISPR-compatible cell wall digestion method: improve autolysin (Shetty et al. 2025: mating at 28 C rather than 18 C) or gentler Alcalase (Walker et al. 2026, diatoms: osmotic support during and after digestion; titrate the enzyme dose down).",
  "Separate delivery failures from editing failures: restore a visual screen to confirm the guides, donor and selection work; include a donor-only, no-RNP control to estimate off-target NHEJ.",
  "Optimize on-target editing: co-target an endogenous selectable marker (Akella et al. 2021; Ross et al. 2024); tune RNP and donor concentrations and ratio (Kim et al. 2020 reached ~37% knock-in)."
 ],
 "openQuestions": [
  "Has anyone gotten Alcalase pretreatment to work in C. reinhardtii? If so, what digestion time, concentration, and recovery conditions did you use?",
  "If you've gotten autolysin preparations working consistently, which factors mattered most?",
  "If we try making targeted knock-ins again, what 'need-to-haves' do you think we missed?"
 ],
